To find \(R_{\text{TH}}\), first calculate the open-circuit voltage \(V_{OC}=V_{\text{TH}}\). <img src="img/bd9d5c8c5a2d61c0.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,256px)"> Applying KCL at node \(V\), \[\frac{V-5}{2k}
+
\frac{V}{2k}
+
\frac{V-3V_{AB}}{1k}
=
0
\tag{1}\] From the dependent source, \[V_{AB}+3V_{AB}=V\] \[V=4V_{AB}
\tag{2}\] Substituting (2) into (1), \[\frac{4V_{AB}-5}{2k}
+
\frac{4V_{AB}}{2k}
+
\frac{V_{AB}}{1k}
=
0\] \[10V_{AB}-5=0\] \[V_{AB}=0.5~\text{V}\] Hence, \[V_{OC}=0.5~\text{V}\] Now short-circuit terminals \(A\) and \(B\). <img src="img/89d1705c9bd2deb9.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,544px)"> Since \(A\) and \(B\) are shorted, \[V_{AB}=0\] Therefore the dependent source voltage is zero and acts as a short circuit. \[\Rightarrow I_{SC}
=
\frac{5}{2k}
=
\frac{2.5}{1000}
=
2.5~\text{mA}\] Hence, \[R_{\text{TH}}
=
\frac{V_{OC}}{I_{SC}}
=
\frac{0.5}{2.5\times10^{-3}}
=
200~\Omega
=
0.2~\text{k}\Omega\] Therefore, \[\boxed{\textbf{B. }0.2~\text{k}\Omega}\]

For a lamp rated \(P\) watts at voltage \(V\), \(R=\dfrac{V^2}{P}\) For a \(100~\text{W},\,220~\text{V}\) lamp, \[R_{100}
=
\frac{220^2}{100}
=
484~\Omega\] Hence the power consumed at \(220\) V is \[P_{100}
=
\frac{220^2}{484}
=
100~\text{W}\] For a \(200~\text{W},\,220~\text{V}\) lamp, \[R_{200}
=
\frac{220^2}{200}
=
242~\Omega\] Let \(n\) such lamps be connected in series. \(\Rightarrow R_{\text{eq}}
=
242n\) Power consumed by the series combination is \[P
=
\frac{220^2}{242n}
=
\frac{200}{n}\] For equal power consumption, \[\frac{200}{n}=100\] \[n=2\] Hence, the correct answer is \[\boxed{\textbf{D. }2}\]

