Given, \[G(s)=\frac{e^{-0.1s}}{s}\] The phase of the frequency response is \[\angle G(j\omega)=-90^\circ-0.1\omega\left(\frac{180^\circ}{\pi}\right)\] At phase crossover frequency, \[\angle G(j\omega_{pc})=-180^\circ\] \[-90^\circ-0.1\omega_{pc}\left(\frac{180^\circ}{\pi}\right)
=-180^\circ\] \[0.1\omega_{pc}\left(\frac{180^\circ}{\pi}\right)=90^\circ\] \[\omega_{pc}=\frac{\pi}{0.2}=5\pi\ \text{rad/s}\] The magnitude at phase crossover frequency is \[|G(j\omega_{pc})|
=\frac{1}{\omega_{pc}}
=\frac{1}{5\pi}\] Hence, the gain margin is \[GM=\frac{1}{|G(j\omega_{pc})|}
=5\pi\] In dB, \[GM_{dB}=20\log_{10}(5\pi)
\approx 23.9\,\text{dB}\] \[\boxed{\text{(D) }23.9\,\text{dB}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not substitute \(-0.1\omega\) directly as degrees. The exponential term \(e^{-j\omega T}\) produces a phase of \(-\omega T \\) radians which must be converted to degrees as \(-\omega T\left(\dfrac{180^\circ}{\pi}\right)\) before equating it to \(-180^\circ\).</li></ul></div>

Given, \[\dot{x}_1=-3x_1+x_2+2u\] \[\dot{x}_2=-2x_2+u\] \[y=x_1\] <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 1</div>Taking Laplace transform with zero initial conditions, \[sX_1(s)=-3X_1(s)+X_2(s)+2U(s)\] \[(s+3)X_1(s)=X_2(s)+2U(s)\] \[sX_2(s)=-2X_2(s)+U(s)\] \[(s+2)X_2(s)=U(s)\] \[X_2(s)=\frac{U(s)}{s+2}\] Substituting in the first equation, \[(s+3)X_1(s)
=
\frac{U(s)}{s+2}+2U(s)\] \[X_1(s)
=
\frac{1+2(s+2)}{(s+2)(s+3)}U(s)\] \[X_1(s)
=
\frac{2s+5}{(s+2)(s+3)}U(s)\] Since \(y=x_1\), \[Y(s)=X_1(s)\] \[\therefore\;
\frac{Y(s)}{U(s)}
=
\frac{2s+5}{s^2+5s+6}\]</div> <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 2</div>Here, \[A=
\begin{bmatrix}
-3 &amp; 1\\
0 &amp; -2
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
B=
\begin{bmatrix}
2\\
1
\end{bmatrix}\] \[C=
\begin{bmatrix}
1 &amp; 0
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
D=0\] Using \[\frac{Y(s)}{U(s)}
=
C(sI-A)^{-1}B+D\] \[sI-A=
\begin{bmatrix}
s+3 &amp; -1\\
0 &amp; s+2
\end{bmatrix}\] \[(sI-A)^{-1}
=
\frac{1}{(s+2)(s+3)}
\begin{bmatrix}
s+2 &amp; 1\\
0 &amp; s+3
\end{bmatrix}\] \[\frac{Y(s)}{U(s)}
=
\frac{1}{(s+2)(s+3)}
\begin{bmatrix}
1 &amp; 0
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
2(s+2)+1\\
s+3
\end{bmatrix}\] \[=
\frac{2s+5}{s^2+5s+6}\]</div> \[\boxed{(C)\;\dfrac{2s+5}{s^2+5s+6}}\]

The state matrix is \[A=
\begin{bmatrix}
-3 &amp; 1\\
0 &amp; -2
\end{bmatrix}\] The state transition matrix is \[\phi(t)=e^{At}\] Using the series expansion, \[e^{At}
=
I+At+\frac{A^2t^2}{2!}+\frac{A^3t^3}{3!}+\cdots\] First, compute the powers of \(A\): \[A^2=
\begin{bmatrix}
9 &amp; -5\\
0 &amp; 4
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
A^3=
\begin{bmatrix}
-27 &amp; 19\\
0 &amp; -8
\end{bmatrix}\] Therefore, \[e^{At}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
1 &amp; 0\\
0 &amp; 1
\end{bmatrix}
+
\begin{bmatrix}
-3 &amp; 1\\
0 &amp; -2
\end{bmatrix}t
+
\begin{bmatrix}
9 &amp; -5\\
0 &amp; 4
\end{bmatrix}
\frac{t^2}{2!}
+
\begin{bmatrix}
-27 &amp; 19\\
0 &amp; -8
\end{bmatrix}
\frac{t^3}{3!}
+\cdots\] \[=
\begin{bmatrix}
1-3t+\dfrac{9t^2}{2!}-\dfrac{27t^3}{3!}+\cdots &amp;
t-\dfrac{5t^2}{2!}+\dfrac{19t^3}{3!}+\cdots\\ 0 &amp;
1-2t+\dfrac{4t^2}{2!}-\dfrac{8t^3}{3!}+\cdots
\end{bmatrix}\] The 1x1 and 2x2 entries are the expansions of \(e^{-3t}\) and \(e^{-2t}\), respectively. The 1x2 entry simplifies to \(e^{-2t}-e^{-3t}.\) Hence, \[\phi(t)=e^{At}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
e^{-3t} &amp; e^{-2t}-e^{-3t}\\
0 &amp; e^{-2t}
\end{bmatrix}\] \[\boxed{(B)\;
\begin{bmatrix}
e^{-3t} &amp; e^{-2t}-e^{-3t}\\
0 &amp; e^{-2t}
\end{bmatrix}}\]

The mutual inductance of a transformer is \[M=\frac{N_{1}N_{2}}{\mathcal{R}}\] where \(\mathcal{R}\) is the total reluctance of the magnetic circuit. Initially, the windings are placed on the two vertical limbs. If they are shifted to the two opposite horizontal limbs, the magnetic flux still completes the same closed iron-core path. <span class="missing-fig">[figure unavailable]</span> The total reluctance of the magnetic circuit remains \[\mathcal{R}
=
2\mathcal{R}_{v}
+
2\mathcal{R}_{h}\] where \[\mathcal{R}_{v}
=
\frac{l_{v}}{\mu A_{v}},
\qquad
\mathcal{R}_{h}
=
\frac{l_{h}}{\mu A_{h}}.\] Although the vertical limbs have a cross-sectional area of \(20\,\text{cm}^{2}\) and the horizontal limbs have \(10\,\text{cm}^{2}\), the magnetic flux still traverses all four limbs in series. Hence, the total reluctance of the magnetic circuit is unchanged. Therefore, \[M=\frac{N_{1}N_{2}}{\mathcal{R}}\] also remains unchanged. \[\boxed{B}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Mutual inductance is inversely proportional to the total magnetic reluctance.</li><li>Shifting the windings to another pair of opposite limbs does not change the magnetic path.</li><li>The entire magnetic circuit determines the reluctance, not merely the limb on which the winding is placed.</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not assume that mutual inductance is proportional only to the cross-sectional area of the limb carrying the winding.</li><li>Do not calculate reluctance using a single limb; the complete magnetic path must be considered.</li><li>Do not conclude that the mutual inductance halves because the horizontal limbs have half the cross-sectional area.</li></ul></div>

The circuit referred to primary looks like as shown below. <span class="missing-fig">[figure unavailable]</span> If \(S\) is open, entire current flows into the magnetizing inductance. <span class="missing-fig">[figure unavailable]</span> From the waveform, \[\left(\frac{di}{dt}\right)_{\max}=2000\] Voltage across magnetizing inductance is \[V=L_m\frac{di}{dt}\] Given, \[L_m=\frac{400}{\pi}\,\mathrm{mH}
=\frac{400}{\pi}\times 10^{-3}\,\mathrm{H}\] Therefore, \[V_p=\left(\frac{400}{\pi}\times 10^{-3}\right)(2000)
=\frac{800}{\pi}\,\mathrm{V}\] Hence, right option is \[\boxed{D}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>For an inductor, \(v=L\dfrac{di}{dt}\).</li><li>With \(S\) open, the full source current flows through \(L_m\).</li><li>Peak voltage occurs at maximum slope of \(i(t)\).</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not forget to convert \(\mathrm{mH}\) into \(\mathrm{H}\).</li><li>Do not use current magnitude; use slope \(\dfrac{di}{dt}\).</li></ul></div>

For the same field excitation of \(20\,\text{A}\), \[E_{oc}=2000\,\text{V}\] and the short-circuit current is \[I_{sc}=400\,\text{A}\] Hence, the synchronous impedance is \[Z_{s}=\frac{E_{oc}}{I_{sc}}
=\frac{2000}{400}
=5\,\Omega\] The internal voltage drop at a load current of \(200\,\text{A}\) is \[\Delta V=I_{a}Z_{s}\] \[\Delta V=200\times 5
=1000\,\text{V}\] Since the machine is operating in the linear region and armature resistance is neglected, Therefore, the correct option is \[\boxed{\text{D}}\]

