The load sharing among parallel generators depends on their voltage regulation (or droop characteristic). Voltage regulation is \[\text{VR}
=
\frac{|E_f|-|V_t|}
{|V_t|}
\times 100\] For generators operating in parallel, the generator having lower regulation experiences a smaller voltage drop with increasing load and therefore takes a larger share of the additional load. Given, \[VR_1=0.02\] \[VR_2=0.04\] \[VR_3=0.03\] Therefore, \[VR_1&lt;VR_3&lt;VR_2\] Since regulation is proportional to the internal impedance (droop), \[Z_1&lt;Z_3&lt;Z_2\] For the same bus-voltage change, \[I_{a1}Z_1
=
I_{a2}Z_2
=
I_{a3}Z_3\] Hence, \[I_{a1}
&gt;
I_{a3}
&gt;
I_{a2}\] Thus Generator-1 supplies the largest increment in load, followed by Generator-3 and then Generator-2. Therefore, \[P_1&gt;P_3&gt;P_2\] for the additional load demand. Hence, the correct answer is \[\boxed{\text{(C) Generator-1 will share more power compared to Generator-2}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Parallel generators share incremental load according to their droop characteristics.</li><li>Lower voltage regulation implies stiffer voltage characteristics.</li><li>A generator with lower regulation picks up a larger portion of an increased load.</li><li>Generator efficiency is not relevant to incremental load sharing in this problem.</li></ul></div>

We match each application with the appropriate compensating device. <strong>(a) Improve power factor</strong><br/>Power factor improvement is achieved by supplying leading reactive power through a shunt capacitor. Before compensation, \[\phi_1
=
\tan^{-1}
\left(
\frac{Q_R}{P_R}
\right)\] \[pf_1=\cos\phi_1\] After shunt-capacitor compensation, \[Q_R=Q_{\text{load}}-Q_C\] \[\phi_2
=
\tan^{-1}
\left(
\frac{Q_{\text{load}}-Q_C}{P_R}
\right)\] \[pf_2=\cos\phi_2\] Since \[\phi_1&gt;\phi_2\] \[pf_2&gt;pf_1\] Therefore, \[(a)\rightarrow(2)\] <img src="img/9900229a78ced31e.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,160px)"> <img src="img/d9b9d5851bd73c8f.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,160px)"> <strong>(b) Reduce current ripples</strong><br/>A series reactor (inductor) opposes sudden current changes and smoothens current variations. \[(b)\rightarrow(4)\] <strong>(c) Increase power flow in a transmission line</strong><br/>Power transfer is \[P
=
\frac{V_SV_R}{X}
\sin\delta\] A series capacitor reduces the effective line reactance \(X\), thereby increasing power transfer capability. \[(c)\rightarrow(3)\] <strong>(d) Reduce Ferranti effect</strong><br/>Ferranti effect is caused by line charging capacitance under light-load conditions. A shunt reactor absorbs the excess reactive power and mitigates the effect. \[(d)\rightarrow(1)\] Thus, \[a\rightarrow2,\quad
b\rightarrow4,\quad
c\rightarrow3,\quad
d\rightarrow1\] Hence, the correct answer is \[\boxed{
\text{(B)}\quad
a\rightarrow2,\;
b\rightarrow4,\;
c\rightarrow3,\;
d\rightarrow1
}\]

Given, \[f=50\,\text{Hz}\] Hence, the time period of the source voltage is \[T=\frac{1}{f}=\frac{1}{50}=20\,\text{ms}\] During the positive half cycle of the source voltage, \[0&lt;t&lt;\frac{T}{2},\] energy is stored in the inductor and the current increases. During the negative half cycle of the source voltage, \[\frac{T}{2}\leq t\leq T,\] the current decreases and the energy stored in the inductor is delivered back to the source. The current through a purely inductive load is \[i(t)=\frac{V_{m}}{\omega L}\left(1-\cos\omega t\right)\] Hence, the maximum current is \[I_{\max}=\frac{2V_{m}}{\omega L}\] Substituting the given values, \[I_{\max}
=\frac{2\times10}
{100\pi\times\left(\dfrac{0.1}{\pi}\right)}\] \[I_{\max}=2\,\text{A}\] \[\boxed{C}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>For a purely inductive load, current lags the applied voltage by \(90^\circ\).</li><li>The current expression is \[i(t)=\frac{V_{m}}{\omega L}\left(1-\cos\omega t\right).\]</li><li>The maximum current occurs at \(\omega t=\pi\), giving \[I_{\max}=\frac{2V_{m}}{\omega L}.\]</li><li>An ideal inductor stores energy during one half cycle and returns it to the source during the next half cycle.</li></ul></div>
